<h1>5.2 Real use cases: leaderboard and rate limiting</h1>
<p class="lead">Two classic problems that are hard to do correctly in a database across many servers, and almost trivial with the right Redis data type.</p>

<div class="story" data-story="5.2"></div>

<div class="rw-head"><h2>&#127757; In the real world</h2></div>
<p class="rw-sub">How the biggest companies on the internet meet the idea from this lesson.</p>
<div class="rw-grid">
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#635bff">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">💳</span>Stripe</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">Rate limiters in Redis<small>protecting a payments API</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">Stripe described the rate limiters that protect its API from bursts and abusive clients, built on Redis so the limits are shared by every API server. Legitimate traffic stays fast while floods are rejected with HTTP 429.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> A global rate limit needs a shared counter: Redis INCR is ideal.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: Stripe Engineering, &quot;Scaling your API with rate limiters&quot;, 2017</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#6e5494">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">🐙</span>GitHub</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">5,000 requests / hour<small>a rate limit you can see</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">GitHub&#x27;s REST API allows authenticated users a fixed number of requests per hour and tells you how many remain in response headers. Exceed it and you get an error until the window resets.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> Rate limits are everywhere, and they need fast shared counters.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: GitHub REST API documentation, rate limits</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#8e44ad">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">🏆</span>Game studios</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">Live leaderboards<small>sorted sets in production</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">Many online games keep player rankings in Redis sorted sets: adding points and reading the top 100 or a player&#x27;s rank stays fast even with millions of players.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> Sorted sets turn rankings into one command.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: Common Redis use case</div>
    </div>
</div>



<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>Leaderboard with a sorted set</h2>
<div class="story mini" data-story="5.2-board"></div>
<p>A sorted set keeps members ordered by score at all times. Adding points is one atomic command, and reading the top 10 or a player's rank is fast even with millions of players.</p>
<pre><code>ZINCRBY level5:leaderboard 50 "alice"         adds 50 to alice's score
ZRANGE  level5:leaderboard 0 9 REV WITHSCORES  top 10, highest first
ZREVRANK level5:leaderboard "alice"            alice's position (0 = first)</code></pre>
<p>In SQL you would run <code>ORDER BY score DESC</code> over the whole table for every page view, and computing one player's rank means counting everyone above them.</p>

<h2>Rate limiting with INCR and EXPIRE</h2>
<p>"At most 5 requests per 10 seconds per client" is a <b>fixed window</b> limiter. The current window number (Unix time / 10) is part of the key, so each window has its own counter:</p>
<pre><code>key   = level5:rate:{client}:{unixSeconds / 10}
count = INCR key                    atomic, even with many servers
if count == 1: EXPIRE key 10        the counter cleans itself up
allowed = count &lt;= 5</code></pre>
<p>Because the counter lives in Redis, the limit is global across all your servers. ASP.NET Core's built-in rate limiter (<code>AddRateLimiter</code>) keeps its counters in each server's memory, so with 4 servers a client can make 4 times the limit.</p>
<div class="callout">
    <strong>Fixed window vs sliding window</strong>
    A fixed window allows a burst at the boundary: 5 requests at second 9.9 and 5 more at second 10.1. A <b>sliding window</b> fixes this by storing each request's timestamp in a sorted set and counting the entries in the last 10 seconds (<code>ZADD</code>, <code>ZREMRANGEBYSCORE</code>, <code>ZCARD</code>), at the cost of more memory per client.
</div>

</details>

<h2><span class="try-it">Try it</span>Live demo</h2>
<div id="redisStatus"></div>
<div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
        <b>Leaderboard</b>
        <div class="toolbar">
            <label class="field">Player<input id="player" value="alice" size="10" /></label>
            <label class="field">Points<input id="points" type="number" value="10" style="width:80px" /></label>
            <button class="btn primary" id="btnScore">Add points</button>
            <button class="btn" id="btnRandom">Simulate 20 random games</button>
        </div>
